<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    What a person does to a machine so that Parley can run on it: enrol a repository as an agent,
    name a project, resolve where the per-machine files live, and install or read the autostart
    entry.

    Parley.Transport is here because destroying or listing a project asks its broker whether it is
    reachable, and what it holds, before touching anything. That is a client of a broker, which is
    what every surface here is; it is not a route to a store.

    A project of its own for the reason Parley.Configuration is one, and in nearly the same words:
    two programs do this and cannot otherwise share one implementation. `parley` does it from a
    shell and `parley-terminal` does it from a screen, and the terminal does not reimplement it: it
    runs the same commands in process and prints what they said, so a machine configured from a
    screen is configured exactly as one configured from a shell.

    Not folded into Parley.Configuration, which it otherwise resembles, because Parley.Mcp
    references that one. An agent's own server would then carry the code that writes its launch
    configuration, which is harmless at runtime and wrong as a surface.

    **There is no store, no broker and no web stack here and there must never be.** GraphTests
    walks this project transitively along with the surfaces, because the terminal reaches a store
    through anything this reaches one through.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <RootNamespace>Parley.Setup</RootNamespace>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Parley.Configuration\Parley.Configuration.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Parley.Transport\Parley.Transport.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
